"Adds responsibility lanes to a procedural flow so that ownership and cross-role handoffs remain visible.\n\nUse when: Investigate delays between departments. Teach who performs each step in a shared operating procedure.\n\nAvoid when: Independent organizations require formal BPMN message and event semantics.\n\nVisual grammar: Parallel lanes denote roles, teams or functions. Action boxes sit wholly inside their responsible lane. Arrows show procedural order and cross-lane handoffs.\n\nBuild: Choose one consistent lane meaning and order lanes by interaction. Place each action in its accountable performing role. Connect decisions and handoffs, adding missing acceptance or rework steps.\n\nCheck: Each action has an unambiguous performing lane. Lane changes correspond to actual handoffs. Decision branches and completion conditions remain complete.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing departments, people and phases as peer lanes. Leaving an action straddling a boundary to avoid resolving ownership.\n\nExample: A purchase request moves from employee preparation to manager approval and procurement ordering, with a rejection path back to the employee.\n\nAlternatives: Flowchart, RACI matrix."
